lized active columns U ̃At\nand non-active columns U ̃Ac\nt ( ̃ju =u j /∥u j ∥2):\nRdec = λdec\n|At| |Ac\nt|\nU ̃ ⊤\nAt\nU ̃Ac\nt\n2\nF\n.(6)\nUnlike all-pairs orthogonalization, Equation (6) leaves correlationswithinA t unconstrained so co-selected channels can cooperatively fit the current task, while repelling their updates from inactive and locked anchors.\n4.3. Trace-Spectral Consolidation & Locking\nStepwise masking protects channels outsideA t, yet an unlocked channel crucial to Session1may still be routed into At during Session4and overwritten. Rather than introducing a separate Fisher pass [2] or post-session SVD [34], RASP harvests a consolidation signal directly from two quantities intrinsic to the routing loop. First, every top-qt selection emits a binary indicator I[j∈ A t], which we accumulate into a cumulative routing trace across all training steps:\ncj ←c j +I[j∈ A t], j= 1, . . . , r.(7)\nSecond, whilec j records how consistently channeljis recruited, it does not measure how strongly atomu jv⊤\nj modifies the frozen backbone. By Proposition2, the exact operator (and nuclear) norm of thej-th rank-1 atom is\nσj = uj v⊤\nj 2 =∥u j ∥2 ∥vj ∥2 .(8)\nWe normalize both quantities to[0,1]via ̃c j =c j/max k ck and ̃σj =σ j/max k σk, and define their geometric mean as thetrace-spectral consolidation index:\nωj =\np\n ̃jc · ̃σj ∈[0,1].(9)\nA channel attains a highω j if and only if it is both frequently selected across tasks and spectrally energetic. Noisy tie-breaker channels ( ̃jc ≫0, ̃σj ≈0) and stale onetask spikes ( ̃jc ≈0, ̃σj ≫0) are automatically suppressed.\nIntra-session Cauchy step attenuation.For unlocked channels selected intoA t, gradients are rescaled inversely by their consolidation index consolidated at the end of sessiont−1:\nguj ← guj\n1 +κ ω (t−1)\nj\n, j∈ A t,(10)\nwhereκ >0is the attenuation gain. Becauseω j already incorporates the square-root geometric compression of Equation (9), the Cauchy multiplier(1 +κ ω j)−1 smoothly decelerates updates on shared high-utility atoms without numerically zeroing their plasticity.\nInter-session boundary anchor locking.At the end of sessiont, we refreshσ j andω (t)\nj . To prevent a single highgradient step in future tasks from corrupting the most critical consolidated atoms, we permanently lock the topm lock\n4\n\n\n Algorithm 1:RASP Training Procedure at Session t\nInput:Shared adapter(U, V), scene gateφ, locked anchor setL t−1, tracec, session dataD t, budget q, gainκ\nOutput:Updated(U, V,L t, c, ω(t))\n//Null-space probe alignment on unlocked pool only Pt−1 ← {1, . . . , r} \\ Lt−1 ;\nProject{v j :j∈ P t−1}onto null-space of prior feature covariance; keep{v j :j∈ L t−1}intact ; foreachminibatch(X, Y)⊂ D t do\nCompute Read-All forward outputhover allratoms via Eq. (1) ; Evaluate cosine probesa j(x), scene gateφ( ̄x), and scoresρ j onP t−1 via Eqs. (2)–(4) ; SelectA t ←Top-q t({ρj :j∈ P t−1})and mask guj = 0forj /∈ At ;\nCompute gradient ofL ce +R dec (Eq. (6)) and apply Cauchy attenuationg uj ←(1 +κ ω (t−1)\nj )−1guj ; Update active synthesis atoms{u j :j∈ A t}and scene gate; accumulatec j ←c j +I[j∈ A t];\nUpdate spectral normsσ j =∥u j∥2 ∥vj∥2 and\nconsolidation indicesω (t)\nj=\np\n ̃jc ̃jσ ; if|P t−1| ≥q+ 2then\nLt ← Lt−1 ∪Top-m lock({ω(t)\nj :j∈ P t−1});\nreturn(U, V,L t, c, ω(t))\nunlocked channels into the anchor setL t:\nLt =L t−1 ∪Top-m lock {ω(t)\nj :j∈ P t−1} ,(11)\nwherem lock = clip(⌊ηlock|Pt−1|⌋,1,3)withη lock = 0.05, and locking halts once|P t−1|< q+ 2to prevent plasticity starvation. Crucially, at the start of sessiont+ 1, only the unlockedinput probes{v j :j∈ P t}are re-initialized in the empirical feature null-space of prior tasks [6], whereas locked pairs{(u j, vj) :j∈ L t}remain completely invariant in both factors. Algorithm1summarizes the complete procedure.\n5. Theoretical Analysis\nWe now formalize why decoupling forward reading from backward writing preserves representational rank while bounding cross-session interference. Full proofs are provided in AppendixA.\nProposition 1(Read-write capacity–interference decoupling).For any active write subsetA t ⊂ {1, . . . , r}of cardinalityq, let∆W sym = 1\nr\nP\nj∈At uj v⊤\nj denote the sym\nmetrically gated adapter. For any inputx∈R d, the forward truncation error incurred by symmetric gating satisfies\n∥(∆W−∆W sym)x∥2 ≤ ∥x∥2\nr\nX\nj /∈ tA\nσj aj(x),(12)\nwhereas RASP incurs zero forward truncation error (∆Wfwd = ∆W). Simultaneously, under isotropic perchannel gradient varianceς 2, RASP’s sparse-plasticity mask reduces expected backward update energy fromrς 2 toqς 2.\nProposition1exposes the structural flaw of symmetric rank routing [12]: zeroingr−qchannels in the forward pass discards a feature residual proportional to the spectral normsσ j of unselected atoms, whereas RASP eliminates (1−q/r)of backward perturbation energy without sacrificing forward rank.\nTheorem 1(Three-tier session drift bound).Consider sessiontwith locked anchor setL t−1 and unlocked poolP t−1. LetS j ⊂ {1, . . . , Mt}denote the minibatch steps at which channelj∈ P t−1 is routed intoA t with step sizes{η s}. Because locked pairs{(u j, vj) :j∈ L t−1}and unlocked probes{v j :j∈ P t−1}remain fixed during sessiont, the functional output shift∆h t(x) = ∆W (t)x−∆W (t−1)xon any probe inputxdecomposes into three tiers:\n∥∆ht(x)∥2 ≤ ∥x∥2\nr\nX\nj∈Pt−1 Sj ̸=∅\n∥vj∥2 aj(x)\n1 +κ ω (t−1)\nj\nX\ns∈Sj\nηs g(s)\nuj 2\n,\n(13) with exact zero drift contributed by locked anchors (j∈ Lt−1) and unrouted unlocked channels (j∈ P t−1, Sj =∅).\nTheorem1guarantees that locked anchors (L t−1) and unrouted unlocked atoms (Sj =∅) contributeexact zero functional drift, while drift on revisited channels is damped by(1 +κ ω (t−1)\nj )−1.\nProposition 2(Spectral identity and conjunctive outlier filtering).Each rank-1 atomM j =u jv⊤\nj satisfies∥M j∥2 = ∥Mj∥∗ =∥u j∥2 ∥vj∥2 =σ j. Moreover, for any locking thresholdτ∈(0,1), the geometric consolidation conditionω j = p ̃jc ̃jσ > τenforces the conjunctive lower\nb