opagation, it reduces the rank of∆W sym fromrtoq, discarding(1−q/r)of the subspace capacity on every inference sample. RASP decouples the two passes: the forward operator has rankr, whereas the backward tangent operator has rankq.\nB.2. Connection to Bilinear Optimization and Subspace Identifiability\nRecent theoretical analyses of continual LoRA training highlight two failure modes: (i)bilinear optimization divergence, where simultaneously updating both low-rank factors(U, V)causes coupled curvature instability across tasks [45,46], and (ii)intruder dimension emergence, where unconstrained rank updates overwrite leading singular directions of pre-trained weights [24,47]. RASP avoids bilinear divergence within each session by holding input probesVfixed during gradient descent and updating only the active synthesis columnsU At . Across sessions, keeping locked pairs{(u j, vj) :j∈ L t}jointly frozen while projecting only unlocked probes{v j :j∈ P t}into the empirical feature null-space ensures that consolidated rank-1 operators are strictly preserved (Corollary1).\nB.3. Why Joint Pair Locking(u j, vj)Is Essential\nA subtle implementation pitfall when combining null-space probe initialization [6] with rank freezing is re-initializing the entire probe matrixVat the start of sessiont+ 1while freezing only the output columnsu j. Because the contribution of\nchanneljto∆W xis the outer productu j(v⊤\nj x), replacingv (t)\nj with a new null-space vectorv (t+1)\nj completely alters the functional map of the “frozen” channelu j, destroying old-task features. As shown in Table5(Part A, Row 4), lockingu j while re-initializingv j drops ImageNet-A accuracy from58.52%to57.63%. Partitioning the null-space projection so that it acts exclusively on unlocked probesP t = [r]\\ L t is therefore mandatory for theoretical and empirical soundness.\nB.4. Biological Inspiration and Scope\nSparse expansion and selective plasticity in neural circuits—such as theDrosophilamushroom body—separate broad sensory readout from localized synaptic updates [21,48]. While this principle motivates decoupling read capacity from write sparsity, RASP does not rely on wide random projections (m= 10 4) [21,48] or the frozen sparse masks of FlyLoRA [12]; instead, it operates inside standard low-rank transformer projections (r≤80) with learned scene-context modulation and exact spectral norm tracking.\nC. Detailed Experimental Settings\nDataset specifications and session splits.Table7summarizes the four benchmarks used in our study. All images are resized to224×224and normalized using standard ImageNet-21K statistics. For every benchmark, we evaluate three random class-ordering seeds (seed∈ {2024,2025,2026}) shared across all compared methods.\n13\n\n\n Table 7. Summary of continual learning benchmarks and incremental session protocols.\nBenchmark Domain Characteristic Classes Train / Test Images SessionsTClasses / Sess. Rankr\nCIFAR-100 [40] Standard natural objects 100 50,000 / 10,000 10 10 50 CUB-200-2011 [41] Fine-grained bird species 200 5,994 / 5,794 10 20 50 ImageNet-R [42] Multi-style artistic renditions 200 24,000 / 6,000 10 / 20 20 / 10 64 ImageNet-A [43] Natural adversarial examples 200 5,981 / 1,499 10 20 80\nBaseline reproduction and unified protocol.To eliminate missing entries in prior comparison tables (where BiLoRA [8] and PLAN [9] omitted CUB-200 and ImageNet-A, and FM-LoRA [27] omitted ImageNet-A), we evaluated all baselines under the same ViT-B/16-IN21K checkpoint (vit base patch16 224 in21k) and identical 3-seed class orders using their official open-source implementations and recommended hyperparameters. For per-session branch-expansion methods (InfLoRA, SD-LoRA, BiLoRA, PLAN, LoRA-P&M, SplitLoRA), each session allocates a rank-10(or matched0.15M parameter) LoRA branch on key and value projections, totaling1.53M adapter parameters afterT= 10sessions.\nFormal metric definitions.Leta t,j ∈[0,100]denote the test accuracy on sessionj’s test setD test\nj i